{"name":"Faience","hex":"#2a6d8b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"faience","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of tin-glazed earthenware ceramic, used domestically for tableware and in architecture as a decorative material.","Beads or small ornaments made from frit, from the eastern Mediterranean of the Bronze and Iron Ages."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/faience"}],"entries":[{"title":"Faience","blocks":[{"text":"Faience or faïence is the general English language term for fine tin-glazed pottery. The invention of a white pottery glaze suitable for painted decoration, by the addition of an oxide of tin to the slip of a lead glaze, was a major advance in the history of pottery. The invention seems to have been made in Iran or the Middle East before the ninth century. A kiln capable of producing temperatures exceeding 1,000 °C (1,830 °F) was required to achieve this result, after millennia of refined pottery-making traditions. The term is now used for a wide variety of pottery from several parts of the world, including many types of European painted wares, often produced as cheaper versions of porcelain styles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Faience_Plate_Traditional.jpg/330px-Faience_Plate_Traditional.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faience"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Potpourri_with_cover_MET_DP230522_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Potpourri_with_cover_MET_DP230522_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Potpourri_with_cover_MET_DP230522_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Potpourri_with_cover_MET_DP230522_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Potpourri_with_cover_MET_DP230522_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Potpourri_with_cover_MET_DP230522_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sophisticated Rococo Niderviller faience, by a French factory that also made porcelain, 1760–65","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APotpourri_with_cover_MET_DP230522_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Berl%C3%ADn_Manises_XV.JPG/500px-Berl%C3%ADn_Manises_XV.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Berl%C3%ADn_Manises_XV.JPG/1280px-Berl%C3%ADn_Manises_XV.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Berl%C3%ADn_Manises_XV.JPG/500px-Berl%C3%ADn_Manises_XV.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hispano-Moresque ware dish from Manises, 15th century, the earliest type of European faience","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABerl%C3%ADn_Manises_XV.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Tureen_Marseille_1770_DMA_1997-46.jpg/500px-Tureen_Marseille_1770_DMA_1997-46.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Tureen_Marseille_1770_DMA_1997-46.jpg/1280px-Tureen_Marseille_1770_DMA_1997-46.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Tureen_Marseille_1770_DMA_1997-46.jpg/500px-Tureen_Marseille_1770_DMA_1997-46.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rococo tureen, Marseille, c. 1770","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATureen_Marseille_1770_DMA_1997-46.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2